{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Arise, walk about the land, through its length and through its breadth.</b> Look to the right and see: for at first the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abraham, ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עוֹמֵד שָׁם צָפוֹנָה וָנֶגְבָּה וָקֵדְמָה וָיָמָּה, כִּי כָל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה רֹאֶה לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״ (Genesis 13:14–15) — hear from this that by sight alone it suffices and he acquires it. But afterward He went back from this and said, ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ לְאָרְכָּהּ וּלְרָחְבָּהּ כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה״ — hear from this that one needs to make an actual חֲזָקָה, possession, for מִכִּי דַּיֵשׁ אַמַצְרֵי (Bava Kama 79b), and by sight alone one does not acquire it. And furthermore: for at first He said ״לְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״, and afterward He said ״כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה״ — and He did not mention זַרְעֲךָ and did not mention ״עַד עוֹלָם״. And furthermore: that He said ״שָׂא נָא״ — נָא is nothing but a request. But in saying ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ״ He did not use the word ״נָא״. And in the case of Moses our master, upon him be peace, the pattern is reversed, for he said (Deuteronomy 3:25) ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא וְאֶרְאֶה אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — behold, he used the word ״נָא״ for the actual crossing, which is the counterpart of ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ״; but for the seeing he did not use ״נָא״, for he did not say ״וְאֶרְאֶה נָּא״. And furthermore: what is the meaning of what is said here, ״וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה עוֹמֵד שָׁם״ — would it enter your mind that he would see from the place where he is not standing?" "And what seems closest to me to say regarding all this is that the Holy One, blessed be He, granted to Abraham the acquisition of the land in order to acquire there two kinds of benefit: one spiritual, acquired by sight alone; and one bodily, acquired by מִכִּי דַּיֵשׁ אַמַצְרֵי. The spiritual one is this: for there is the place of the Temple below, aligned opposite the Temple on high, and there HaShem established a dwelling for His seat, blessed be He, and there the hiding of His might, blessed be He, resides. And whoever gazes upon that holy place is immediately clothed in a spirit of purity and holiness, and his eyes behold the supernal King in His beauty, blessed be He, and by sight alone it suffices for a man to acquire that perfection in the place which Abraham called (Genesis 22:14) ״ה׳ יִרְאֶה״ — and it is read ״יִרְאֶה״ with a yod in ḥiriq and ״יֵרָאֶה״ with a yod in tzere, for just as he comes to see, so does He come to be seen; for immediately upon arriving there, just as the Shekhinah sees him, so he sees the face of the Shekhinah, and immediately he is influenced and anointed and cleaves to the radiance of His Shekhinah, blessed be He, from the wellspring of the World to Come — for there too the righteous sit and enjoy the radiance of His Shekhinah, blessed be He. And a man does not merit this perfection in every place in the land, but only in the place called ״ה׳ יִרְאֶה״, which is Mount Moriah and Bethel — for above it is written (Genesis 13:3–4) ״עַד בֵּית אֵל, אֶל מְקוֹם הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ etc., and further on in the portion of Vayetze (Genesis 28:17), Rashi explained that [Jacob] came to the Temple to greet Him as far as Bethel, and the commentators explained that from that time onward the two places were joined — the place of the Temple and Bethel — and became one place. Therefore it is said here to Abram, ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם״ — pointing with a finger, that this spiritual perfection acquired by sight alone is acquired only from the place where he stands, namely Bethel. For thus the plain sense of the verses implies: ״וַיֵּלֶךְ לְמַסָּעָיו מִנֶּגֶב עַד בֵּית אֵל״ etc. And this is what He said: ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַרְאֶךָּ״ — that I am showing you there, and this is the reason: ״מֶלֶךְ בְּיָפְיוֹ תֶּחֱזֶינָה עֵינֶיךָ״." "And this spiritual benefit He will not remove from his seed forever, for even at a time when the Temple below is not in its built state, nevertheless the Temple on high, aligned opposite it, is eternal and will never be removed. And at all times the influence descends from it upon the holy seed of Abraham. And for this reason, many perfect ones — and thus many — their soul longs to stand in that holy place, ״כִּי רָצוּ אֶת אֲבָנֶיהָ״ etc. (Psalms 102:15). And corresponding to this spiritual benefit, the Holy One, blessed be He, said, ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ וּרְאֵה מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם״ (Genesis 13:14) — that is to say: from the holy place where you stand, for in it you shall acquire the spiritual perfection and merit to see the face of the Shekhinah. And concerning that perfection He said, ״לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה וּלְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״ (Genesis 13:15), for this spiritual perfection shall never depart. And afterward He said, corresponding to the bodily perfection acquired in the land — namely, an ordinary inheritance of field and vineyard — and an ordinary piece of land is not acquired except by full possession, מִכִּי דַּיֵשׁ אַמַצְרֵי. He said, ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ לְאָרְכָּהּ וּלְרָחְבָּהּ כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה״ (Genesis 13:17), and He did not mention ״לְזַרְעֲךָ עַד עוֹלָם״ — for the land was given to Israel conditionally: if they keep His statutes and teachings." "And concerning the acquisition of these two kinds of perfection, Moses said, ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא וְאֶרְאֶה אֶת הָאָרֶץ הַטּוֹבָה״ (Deuteronomy 3:25). For in saying ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא״ he asked for the acquisition of an ordinary portion in the land, for which actual crossing is required, in the manner that was said, ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 13:17). And in saying ״וְאֶרְאֶה אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ he asked for the acquisition of the spiritual perfection acquired by sight alone. And corresponding to what is said here, ״מִן הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה שָׁם״ (Genesis 13:14) — namely Bethel, the place of the Temple — he said there, ״הָהָר הַטּוֹב הַזֶּה וְהַלְּבָנוֹן״ (Deuteronomy 3:25). And as for what is written here, ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ״ (Genesis 13:14), the word ״נָא״ is used specifically for that perfection acquired by sight; but for the bodily perfection, the word ״נָא״ is not used. And in the case of Moses the opposite is written — but it is all one rationale: that the bodily perfection, a man himself desires it visibly, more than anything; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, does not need to ask the man, in language of supplication, to receive it from His hand — but on the contrary, the man asks for it, in language of supplication, from the Holy One, blessed be He, for it is a desire before the eyes to see in the desirability of this world. But the spiritual desire, of which ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה״ (Isaiah 64:3), the nature is not so drawn toward it, and man does not ask for it in language of supplication according to his nature — but on the contrary, HaShem asks man, in language of supplication, to cleave to this spiritual perfection. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abram, ״שָׂא נָא״ — נָא is nothing but request and supplication, that HaShem asked him to cleave to this spiritual acquisition. But ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ״ in order to acquire an inheritance of field and vineyard — there was no need to ask him. But Moses, when he asked for both of these from the Holy One, blessed be He, used the word ״נָא״ in saying ״אֶעְבְּרָה״, that is, the acquisition of a portion in the land, toward which nature is drawn and which one requests accordingly; but in saying ״וְאֶרְאֶה אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ he did not use the word ״נָא״ — for it is all one rationale. And even though this is not fitting to say regarding two righteous men such as Abraham and Moses, nevertheless the Torah spoke in the language of ordinary human beings, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abram ״שָׂא נָא״ and ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ״ as one speaking to an ordinary man, and Moses too did not wish to hold himself as a perfect righteous man." "Another matter: Abraham did not have haughty eyes and he would constantly keep his gaze downward; therefore it was said to him, ״שָׂא נָא עֵינֶיךָ״ (Genesis 13:14). ״נָא״ means ״now״ — for now, for the moment, lift up your eyes and see."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:48.200980429Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}